{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_014RYGNZS2fb8LEQUvCnbvvM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Written and committed `answers/01-014-how-does-chunking-happen.md` — Q14: **How does chunking happen?**\n\nThe note covers:\n\n- **Four strategies** ranked by sophistication: fixed-size → recursive (LangChain `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter`) → semantic (embedding-similarity splits, `chonkie`) → parent-child hierarchical (LlamaIndex `ParentDocumentRetriever`)\n- **Tradeoff table**: small chunks (high embedding precision, bigger index) vs. large chunks (richer generation context, blurred embeddings)\n- **Edge cases**: tables must stay intact or become structured Markdown/JSON, lists chunk at item level, cross-page metadata (e.g., \"amounts in thousands\") injected as chunk metadata rather than relied on via retrieval\n- **Production benchmark**: 512→256 token recursive chunks improved retrieval recall@5 from 61%→74% on a 50K-article support RAG\n- **Three concrete pitfalls**: no overlap on fixed-size, one-size-fits-all strategy, ignoring embedding model `max_seq_length` causing silent truncation\n\nNext up: Q15 — *What is the difference between discriminative and generative models?*"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":322,"cache_read_input_tokens":58344,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":322,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5872e373-e123-42c7-9747-c0b305261e66","uuid":"1770f249-2c85-4cea-9b69-ae25d68eacfb"}
